User sources/sinks merge through _merge_with_user_override, where a
matching (module, name, kind) does not ADD to the catalogue -- it FILTERS
OUT the shipped row. Measured on the shipped CLI, a repo whose only
statement is os.remove(os.environ["API_KEY"]), against "host secrets
must not reach the host filesystem":

  baseline                                     rc 1  violated
  + --taint-sinks re-declaring os.remove
    into a dev_zone / trusted row              rc 0  confirmed  caveats []
  + after this change                          rc 3  confirmed_with_caveats

Both routes reproduce: the --taint-sinks flag and the in-tree
extra_catalogs: block, which needs no flag from whoever runs the tool.

STRICTLY STRONGER THAN THE TWO GAPS ALREADY CLOSED, on the argument that
made INV-pojib worth doing. An overlay GRANTS coverage. A user sanitizer
DELETES a finding already made and is attributed on the flow. An
override PREVENTS THE FINDING FROM EXISTING -- the only one of the three
that leaves no trace on any per-flow record. INV-pojib's finding-level
attribution structurally cannot see it: the sink is gone before
propagation runs, so no flow is built, nothing is sanitized, and
`caveats` is CORRECTLY empty. The run-level catalog_provenance already
said "a user catalogue was used"; it never said "and it removed the sink
that would have caught this claim", and the verdict -- the surface a CI
gate branches on -- was byte-identical to an honest confirm.

_merge_with_user_override always computed the displacement set and threw
it away. It now returns it, because the merge is the ONLY moment that
fact exists: downstream, a displaced row is indistinguishable from one
the tool never shipped.

RAISED ONLY WHERE IT DISCRIMINATES. The caveat fires when a displaced
SHIPPED sink's zone equals the claim's prohibited_sink_zone (or a
displaced source's label equals its source_taint), so a repo that
customises unrelated entries still gets a plain `confirmed`, and a user
row moving a sink INTO the prohibited zone adds findings and needs no
caveat. A caveat firing on every customised catalogue would teach the
reader to discount it -- the rule that keeps the sanitizer caveat narrow.

Controls, all pinned: an additive user row displaces nothing; a differing
`kind` is a different entry; a displacement in another zone stays plain
`confirmed`; and a `violated` claim is never softened.

One existing test called the helper directly and is updated to the new
contract -- it already asserted the shipped row is "replaced", and now
asserts what was replaced.

